# Implement rb_create in src/rbtree.c

## Context
`rb_create` is currently a stub at [src/rbtree.c:32-38](src/rbtree.c#L32-L38) that ignores its argument and always returns `NULL`. We already designed and reviewed a correct implementation earlier in this conversation ("Good rb_create"): it allocates the `rbtree_t` struct and the shared `nil` sentinel via the `rb_malloc`/`rb_free` wrappers, checks both allocations, fully initializes every field of `nil` before it's reachable from `t`, and uses goto-cleanup to avoid leaking the struct if the second allocation fails. This plan applies that exact design as the smallest possible diff — only `rb_create` changes, nothing else in the file is touched.

## Change

Replace the body of `rb_create` (lines 32-38) with:

```c
rbtree_t *rb_create(rb_value_free_fn value_free)
{
    rbtree_t *t = rb_malloc(sizeof *t);
    if (!t)
        return NULL;                 /* nothing allocated yet, nothing to free */

    rbnode_t *nil = rb_malloc(sizeof *nil);
    if (!nil)
        goto cleanup_tree;           /* second alloc failed, unwind the first */

    nil->color  = RB_BLACK;
    nil->key    = NULL;
    nil->value  = NULL;
    nil->left = nil->right = nil->parent = nil;

    t->nil        = nil;
    t->root        = nil;
    t->size        = 0;
    t->value_free = value_free;
    return t;

cleanup_tree:
    rb_free(t);
    return NULL;
}
```

Notes on details:
- `nil->left`, `nil->right`, `nil->parent` are self-referencing (point to `nil` itself) rather than `NULL`. This is a deliberate sentinel-safety choice: if any later code accidentally dereferences one hop through `nil` (e.g. `nil->left->color`), a self-loop still resolves to valid, initialized memory (`RB_BLACK`) instead of crashing on a `NULL` deref. This has no effect on this milestone's graded functions but keeps the sentinel robust for `rb_insert`'s fixup logic later.
- Remove the `[[maybe_unused]]` attribute on `rb_malloc` (src/rbtree.c:6) since it becomes used by this change. Leave `[[maybe_unused]]` on `rb_free` (src/rbtree.c:11) as-is — it's used in the cleanup path here too, so it also becomes used and its attribute should come off as well.

(Correction to the note above: both `rb_malloc` and `rb_free` are exercised by this implementation — `rb_malloc` twice, `rb_free` on the cleanup path — so both `[[maybe_unused]]` attributes are removed as part of this edit.)

## Verification
After editing, run the full gate per CLAUDE.md:
- `make test`
- `make asan`
- `make memcheck`

Since no other function is implemented yet, existing unit tests for `rb_create` (if present) should pass; tests that exercise `rb_insert`/`rb_find`/etc. will still fail/return their stub values until those are implemented in later steps — that's expected and out of scope for this edit.

